<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hannah Arendt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Johanna Arendt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hannah Arendt was a German and American philosopher and political theorist, one of the most influential of the twentieth century. Born to a Jewish family in Linden, she studied under Martin Heidegger and took her doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1929. Briefly imprisoned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> in 1933, she fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, escaped detention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1941. The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951) established her reputation, followed by The Human Condition (1958) and by On Revolution and Eichmann in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> (1963), whose phrase the banality of evil stirred controversy. She died in 1975, leaving The Life of the Mind unfinished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt.jpg" alt="Hannah Arendt" width="330" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barbara Niggl Radloff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannah_Arendt_auf_dem_1._Kulturkritikerkongress,_Barbara_Niggl_Radloff,_FM-2019-1-5-9-16_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
